Key Points

• The word &quot;pattern&quot; is typically used with the preposition &quot;in&quot; to indicate the arrangement or design of objects.

• Option 4, &quot;The chef has arranged the cherries in a pattern,&quot; correctly uses the collocation &quot;in a pattern.&quot;

• Options 1, 2, and 3 incorrectly use prepositions (&quot;by,&quot; &quot;of,&quot; or &quot;with&quot;) which do not align with the standard collocation for &quot;pattern.&quot;

Therefore, the correct answer is Option 4.

Correct Sentence: &quot;The chef has arranged the cherries in a pattern.&quot;

Additional Information

• Option 1: &quot;by a pattern&quot; - The preposition &quot;by&quot; is incorrect in this context as it does not convey arrangement or design.

• Option 2: &quot;of a pattern&quot; - The preposition &quot;of&quot; is incorrect here because it implies possession rather than arrangement.

• Option 3: &quot;with a pattern&quot; - The preposition &quot;with&quot; is incorrect as it does not align with the standard collocation for &quot;pattern.&quot;
